WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:08.750
MSR-3D: Multi-Mode Semantic Representation for Open-Vocabulary 3D Scene Understanding.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:14.046
Its key idea: see every 3D object in many ways, not just one.

00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:18.823
This scene is made of about half a million 3D Gaussians.

00:00:18.983 --> 00:00:24.479
The task: find objects in a 3D scene just by describing them.

00:00:24.639 --> 00:00:30.783
Given a free-form query, such as “toy elephant”, the model selects the matching 3D instances.

00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:35.365
Across viewpoints, the same object can look very different.

00:00:35.525 --> 00:00:40.325
Occlusion and incomplete masks change the semantic evidence we observe.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:47.682
Existing methods typically compress these multi-view observations into a single semantic descriptor,

00:00:47.842 --> 00:00:51.322
which cannot represent the complete 3D instance.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:56.930
MSR-3D instead keeps several semantic modes per instance.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:03.426
Each mode summarizes a group of similar views with a representative feature and statistical attributes.

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:08.982
For each candidate 3D instance, we first build a raw observation pool.

00:01:09.142 --> 00:01:14.542
2D-segmentation observations use SAM masks predicted on the original images.

00:01:15.483 --> 00:01:21.603
3D-segmentation observations instead project the segmented 3D instance into each view.

00:01:21.763 --> 00:01:28.363
In this occluded view, the SAM mask sees only a fragment, while the rendered mask still covers the whole instance.

00:01:28.523 --> 00:01:34.379
Both are encoded from the same cropped RGB context by a shared mask-conditioned encoder.

00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:40.501
The two sources are complementary, and both are collected across the training views.

00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:44.357
Their view-wise features are retained rather than averaged.

00:01:45.283 --> 00:01:49.243
The pool is then lifted into a compact representation.

00:01:49.403 --> 00:01:55.211
Each prototype summarizes a group of nearby features and represents one semantic mode.

00:01:56.150 --> 00:02:06.686
Prototypes are chosen by three query-independent rules: a reference built from the most complete views, coverage that weighs 2D and 3D evidence equally, and diversity.

00:02:06.846 --> 00:02:09.006
They stay fixed during matching.

00:02:09.917 --> 00:02:18.221
Each prototype also carries statistical attributes, such as its support, its source composition, and its compactness,

00:02:18.381 --> 00:02:21.309
which indicate how reliable the mode is.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:29.594
Three instance-level attributes, the prototype count, dominant support and source agreement, summarize the whole instance.

00:02:29.754 --> 00:02:34.122
Together, they form the multi-mode semantic representation.

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:40.618
At query time, the text feature is compared with every prototype of each candidate instance.

00:02:40.778 --> 00:02:48.722
The mode-aware matcher combines these similarities with the statistical attributes into a three-dimensional score for each query–instance pair.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:55.962
Learned decision boundaries in this score space separate true matches from semantically related distractors.

00:02:56.122 --> 00:03:00.154
For the query knife, all five selected instances are knives:

00:03:00.314 --> 00:03:04.682
the answer is a set of 3D instances, not a single best match.

00:03:05.617 --> 00:03:08.185
Here are more qualitative results.

00:03:08.345 --> 00:03:13.025
In the ramen scene, the query nori selects the corresponding 3D instance.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:19.302
In the bench scene, orange cat identifies the target among nearby objects.

00:03:20.217 --> 00:03:23.769
In the room scene, dinosaur selects the toy dinosaur.

00:03:23.929 --> 00:03:29.737
Each selection is a set of reconstructed Gaussians, so it can be inspected from any viewpoint.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:36.962
We report scene-averaged mIoU, mAcc and mF1 against eight compared methods.

00:03:37.122 --> 00:03:47.274
On LERF67, MSR-3D reaches 64.70% mIoU and 72.63% mF1,

00:03:47.434 --> 00:03:52.498
6.69 and 9.81 points above the best compared method.

00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:58.505
On LERF45, whose query strings never appear in the training supervision,

00:03:58.665 --> 00:04:02.049
MSR-3D leads in all three metrics.

00:04:02.983 --> 00:04:15.079
On 3DOVS61, ten scenes with sixty-one scene–query entries, it reaches 80.98% mIoU and 83.02% mF1,

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:18.047
again the best of all compared methods.

00:04:18.983 --> 00:04:27.623
MSR-3D turns complementary observations into multiple semantic modes, then learns to combine their evidence for instance selection.

00:04:27.783 --> 00:04:32.871
Code, pretrained parameters and data are available on our project page.
